ad of around five to six points: the district's R+7 lean and incumbency outweigh a favorable Democratic environment and Nez's solid fundraising. Polling is sparse and partisan on both sides, ranging from a two-point GBAO deficit to a double-digit Peak Insights gap, so a Lean R call with wide uncertainty fits the evidence.", "watch": "A second independent poll confirming the narrow GBAO margin, or outside spending flooding the race, could move this toward Tossup or back toward Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Eli Crane (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Eli Crane seeks a third term in this northeastern Arizona district, which leans Republican by about seven points. He enters with a large fundraising edge, roughly $9.5 million raised and $2.8 million on hand, though Democratic outside groups have spent modestly against him. Former Navajo Nation President Jonathan Nez has raised $3.5 million and is running a rematch campaign focused on water issues and costs.\n\nPolling is limited and sponsor-skewed: a Democratic-sponsored August poll showed Crane up two, while a Republican-sponsored poll showed a double-digit lead. Markets price Nez's chances around a third, and most raters call the race Likely R, with two at Lean R. The forecast lands on a Crane lead of about five points with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1703, "margin": -5.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -5.5}]}